<div class="titleband" data-screen-label="The Case — title"><h1>The <span class="u-1">Case</span></h1></div>
<div class="introband">
  <h2>The receipts behind <span class="u-1">every claim</span> we make.</h2>
  <p>The Case is Uncost&rsquo;s evidence layer — built to be useful to supporters, journalists, researchers, urban planners, funders, and skeptics alike. It covers what humans need, what those needs cost by region, where automation and shared infrastructure can plausibly help, and the open methodology behind every claim.</p>
  <p class="u-11"><b>Want the whole argument in one document?</b> Download <em>The Case for Uncost</em> &mdash; the fourteen-page paper this page distills, with every figure&rsquo;s exact reference. <a href="/downloads/the-case-for-uncost.pdf">Download the Case for Uncost (PDF)</a></p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION: case.The mechanism -->
<section class="blk blk--first" data-screen-label="The mechanism">
  <div class="eyebrow">The mechanism</div>
  <h2 class="sec">The technology that could make living cheaper is instead making ownership more valuable.</h2>
  <p class="u-11 lead">Automation is extraordinarily effective at cutting the cost of producing things &mdash; that&rsquo;s not in dispute. The question is who receives the benefit when it does. When a company automates, its costs fall and its value rises, and that value accrues to whoever owns the company. Ownership itself is concentrated: the bottom half of American households collectively hold about 1% of the stocks and funds owned outside retirement accounts. The wealthiest 1% hold about half. On the broader measure &mdash; total household net worth &mdash; the top 1% still hold roughly thirty per cent.</p>
  {{ fig.stat(register.byId["SRC-020"]) }}
  {{ fig.stat(register.byId["SRC-024"]) }}
  <p class="u-11">Nobody is doing the deliberate work of routing automation&rsquo;s gains back toward the price of rent, groceries, and electricity instead of the value of assets. That&rsquo;s not an accusation that anyone is doing something wrong &mdash; a system optimising for one outcome doesn&rsquo;t accidentally produce a different one. It&rsquo;s why Uncost exists: to measure where a specific technology could plausibly lower a specific cost, publish the method, and hand it to the people who can act on it.</p>
</section>

